  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Massive FAQ: Capital Gains Tax Answered</h2>
  <div class="space-y-4 text-body dark:text-muted mb-8">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-bold">What exactly is capital gains tax?</h3>
      <p>Capital gains tax is the tax levied on the profit you earn when you sell an asset like stocks, mutual funds, or real estate for more than what you paid for it.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-bold">How is STCG different from LTCG?</h3>
      <p>STCG (Short-Term Capital Gains) applies to assets held for a short period (e.g., less than 1 year for stocks), while LTCG (Long-Term Capital Gains) applies to assets held longer. LTCG usually has more favorable tax rates and may offer indexation benefits.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-bold">Can I offset my capital losses against my salary income?</h3>
      <p>No, capital losses cannot be offset against salary income. They can only be offset against capital gains (with specific rules regarding STCG and LTCG).</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-bold">How does indexation work?</h3>
      <p>Indexation allows you to adjust the purchase price of an asset for inflation over the years you held it, effectively reducing your taxable profit. It uses the Cost Inflation Index (CII) provided by the government.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-bold">Are there ways to avoid paying LTCG on property?</h3>
      <p>Yes! You can claim exemptions under Sections 54, 54F, or 54EC by reinvesting your gains into another residential property or specified government bonds.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

